1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,536
If the smallest tradable unit breaks your risk limit, the disciplined position size may be zero.

2
00:00:06,536 --> 00:00:07,762
Size comes last.

3
00:00:07,762 --> 00:00:12,989
Start by deciding the amount you are prepared to risk if the stop is reached.

4
00:00:12,989 --> 00:00:16,474
That amount sets the budget before you calculate any size.

5
00:00:16,474 --> 00:00:19,300
Measure the distance from entry to the stop.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:25,304
That distance is only one part of the calculation; it is not the loss amount by itself.

7
00:00:25,304 --> 00:00:32,356
Multiply the stop distance by the instrument's point or tick value to estimate planned loss per

8
00:00:32,356 --> 00:00:32,797
unit.

9
00:00:32,797 --> 00:00:35,882
Check contract rules and minimum size increments.

10
00:00:35,882 --> 00:00:39,702
Divide the planned risk amount by the loss per unit.

11
00:00:39,702 --> 00:00:41,994
Round down to the permitted increment.

12
00:00:41,994 --> 00:00:46,578
If even one unit exceeds the plan, passing is a valid decision.

13
00:00:46,578 --> 00:00:51,121
This is a planning estimate, not a guaranteed loss cap.

14
00:00:51,121 --> 00:00:58,845
For a standard stop order, the SEC says the trigger becomes a market order; the execution price

15
00:00:58,845 --> 00:00:59,754
can differ.

16
00:00:59,754 --> 00:01:04,752
Paper-trading education only; not live-money performance; not investment advice; no guaranteed

17
00:01:04,752 --> 00:01:05,206
returns.
